I"-<p>Marine biogeography is often described using regional frameworks that divide the ocean into provinces, ecoregions, or assessment regions. These maps provide useful context, but plankton habitats are also shaped by moving gradients in salinity, nutrients, light, temperature, and water-column structure. We study these habitats as dynamic biological-environmental states rather than fixed boundaries on a map, drawing on work with the <a href="https://montoyalab.gatech.edu/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Montoya Lab at Georgia Tech</a>.</p>
